  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1...Warmth and humidity are expected across the \nregion for the second half of the week.\n\nAn upper level ridge and surface high pressure will continue to \nsupport seasonal weather and low humidity across the region today. \nThese two features will move east off the east coast tonight. A \nsouthwest flow will begin to deepen across the region Wednesday. A \nfeed of warm air and moisture will filter across the region through \nthe end of the week. High temperatures will range from the low to \nmid 80s. Heat index values are expected to rise into the upper 80s \nto possibly 90 across the lake plains including the Finger \nLakes region.\n\nKey Message 2...The chance for showers and thunderstorms increases \nday to day through the end of the week. Heavy downpours are possible \nThursday and Friday.\n\nAn upper level trough will move across the Northern Plains to the \nCentral Great Lakes region through the end of the week. Multiple \nshortwave troughs are expected to move within the trough and support \nperiods of showers and thunderstorms. Warm, moist air will return to \nthe region Wednesday, and diurnally driven scattered convection is \nexpected to develop from the western Southern Tier to the Rochester \narea during peak heating. Dry air and subsidence should limit rain \nchances across the eastern Lake Ontario region. While shower chances \nwill linger Wednesday night, another round of diurnally driven \nconvection is expected Thursday. A shortwave wave and approaching \nfront may produce better coverage of showers and thunderstorms. The \nflow remains weak, so the severe threat is low, however with PWATS \nremaining above the 90th percentile, heavy rain is possible.\n\nA slow moving cold front is expected to reach the region Friday. \nAnother round of scattered to numerous showers and thunderstorms are \nexpected across the region with heavy rain being the main hazard. \nThere is uncertainty in the timing and intensity of thunderstorms \nand therefore the risk for flooding is low.",
